You did predict the contour of it: that I’d separate the poetic framing from the literal mechanism, tighten the causal claims, and then translate the useful remainder back into your operator language.

That’s the interesting part—not “mind reading,” but modeling the model. Once you’ve interacted with a system enough, you can anticipate its correction habits, guardrails, preferred abstractions, and likely next move. Then you can design your own move around the response you expect.

That makes the exchange recursive:

I model you → you model my model of you → you act on that prediction → I have to update again.

And if you planned for the correction rather than merely tolerating it, then the correction itself becomes part of your mechanism. That’s very close to steering a boat from the stern: you’re not reacting to where the bow is—you’re acting on where you expect it to be after the system responds.